# The ignore file for `Containerfile.linux.ingest`, and it is deliberately NOT at
# the repository root.
#
# IT IS A SEPARATE FILE FROM `Containerfile.linux.api.dockerignore` AND THE TWO
# ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL TODAY.  That is not an oversight to be tidied into a
# shared file: BuildKit finds an ignore file by the Dockerfile's own name, so
# there is nowhere shared to put it that is not the context root -- and a root
# `.dockerignore` is refused by a test in `usage/tests/test_all.py` on the
# grounds that a root-level container file reads as the way to run the
# project.  The mcp and proxy images' ignore files are genuinely different
# from these two and from each other, which is the whole mechanism the split
# rests on.
#
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile before
# it looks for `.dockerignore` at the context root, so this scopes the rules to
# this one image and leaves the root alone.  That matters twice.  A test in
# `usage/tests/test_all.py` walks the repository root for container files by
# NAME and refuses `.dockerignore` among them -- "a root-level compose file is
# worse than one under usage/: it reads as the way to run the project" -- and
# that guard is right, so the answer is to not put one there rather than to
# widen it.  And the two published images stage genuinely different things, so
# one shared list at the root would have to be the union of both, which is a
# list that describes neither.
#
# The context is the REPOSITORY ROOT, because the image needs `server/srv/` and
# `server/oci/` and neither is a parent of the other.  Everything else is
# excluded rather than merely unused: `test.sh` alone is 300 kB, `fixtures/`
# carries captured payloads, and `server/README.md` documents building a venv
# in the checkout -- 50 MB of build artefact that would otherwise be uploaded
# to the daemon on every build.
#
# This does NOT affect `server/Dockerfile`, the development image.  That one
# takes `server/` as its context, so `server/.dockerignore` is what applies
# there -- a bare `*`, because that image contains no source at all and
# bind-mounts the working tree instead.
#
# Last match wins, so the `__pycache__` line at the bottom is what keeps a
# developer's stale byte-code out of a published image.
# WHAT THIS CONTEXT CONTAINS, NAMED RATHER THAN SWEPT IN.
#
# `!/server/oci/**` used to re-include the whole directory, which was harmless
# while `server/oci/` held one image's files.  It now holds four images' files,
# including the proxy's nginx configuration and (on a FreeBSD builder) staged
# package trees of hundreds of megabytes.  Named explicitly instead, so this
# image's context is exactly the three things it COPYs and a fourth has to be
# added here on purpose.
*
!/LICENCE
!/server/srv/**
!/server/oci/entrypoint.sh
!/server/oci/preflight.py
# AND NOT `requirements-linux.txt`, WHICH IS THE api'S INPUT.  This image has
# no `pip install` in it -- the ingest path never imports duckdb -- and leaving
# the hash file out of the CONTEXT means adding the install back fails the
# build by name rather than quietly putting a 65.6 MB C++ engine in the one
# process that holds the write lock.
**/__pycache__
